    <h1>Pandoc/Quarto math quote render test</h1>
    <p>Select part of the definition below, including a formula, and save the annotation.
      Two different values come out of that. The browser's capture goes into the annotation's
      <code>TextQuoteSelector</code> and is used only for anchoring; this fixture loads no
      MathJax, so that capture is the literal <code>\(…\)</code> markup you see below, where
      on the published Pandoc document it would be the flattened rendering
      <code>ιA:C.A→C&nbsp;…</code>. Separately, at intake, h's normalization service fetches
      this page server-side, extracts the authored TeX from its
      <code>&lt;span class="math"&gt;</code> markup, and stores it as the annotation's
      <code>normalized_quote</code> (<code>method='html'</code>). The sidebar displays that
      stored value and nothing else: it does no reconstruction, recovery, or OCR of its own
      when the annotation is shown, and it never reads the selector of a saved annotation.</p>

    <p><strong>What to look for.</strong> The sidebar quote should <em>typeset</em> the
      formulas that appear below only as <code>\(…\)</code> source — a subscripted
      <code>ι_A</code> and a script <code>𝒞</code> — sitting inline within the sentence
      rather than each breaking onto its own line. The failures:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A red normalization-error banner in place of the quote: the API response carried no
        <code>normalized_quote</code>, so there is no <code>annotation_normalized</code> row.</li>
      <li><code>\(…\)</code> shown as plain text instead of typeset math: either the sidebar's
        math rendering did not run over the stored value, or the stored value is a legacy raw
        row (<code>method</code> of <code>raw</code> or <code>identity</code>). Read
        <code>normalized_quote</code> and <code>method</code> off the API response to tell
        those apart, and run h's normalization reconciliation for the latter.</li>
      <li>Each formula on its own line, breaking up the sentence: the quote's math is
        typeset but the sidebar stylesheet is laying inline math out as display math.</li>
      <li>Formulas that typeset but do not match the definition: intake-time extraction
        recovered the wrong source range, or fell back to OCR of the rendered region.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- Real pandoc source, verbatim from the published framework doc. The math is authored as
         <span class="math inline">\(TeX\)</span> / <span class="math display">\[TeX\]</span>. -->
    <p><span class="theorem-title"><strong>Definition 2.4 (Intersection of classifiers)</strong></span> For classifiers <span class="math inline">\(\iota_A \colon \mathcal C.A \to \mathcal C\)</span> and <span class="math inline">\(\iota_B \colon \mathcal C.B \to \mathcal C\)</span> their intersection is the pullback in <span class="math inline">\(\mathbf{Cat}\)</span> <span class="math display">\[
\mathcal C.A \times_{\mathcal C} \mathcal C.B \longrightarrow \mathcal C,
\]</span> the category of objects carrying both structures compatibly. It is again a classifier. Iterating gives the intersection of any finite family.</p>

